#|

In this example, we want to define a new kind of generic function which can
have only one method. So, adding a new method to the generic function will
delete its previous method (if it exists).  This permits us to reuse the
powerful method selection mechanism to check parameters as in the
define-checked syntax of SRFI-273.

We will see that we need to define a new kind of generic for that, and that
the standard <generic> class is not adequate here.

Suppose for instance that we define

    (define-method log ([msg <string>] value)
       (eprintf "*** ~A: ~W~%" msg value))

Now let's try using log:

    stklos> (log "Hello" 10)
    *** Hello: 10           ;; Fine
    stklos> (log 10 10)
    1                       ;; not an error here

The second call does not produce an error because, in STklos, when a method
would overwrite an existing function, it will first add the old function as a
fallback to the generic function. We can see this using describe:

stklos> (describe log)
log is a generic function. It's an instance of <generic>.
Methods defined for log
    Method #[<method> 7f578880aa80]
        Specializers: (<string> <top>)
    Method #[<method> 7f578880aab0]
        Specializers: <top>

We see here that the <generic> log has two methods associated. To circumvent
this issue, we will define a new kind of generic called <unique-generic>. When
a method is added to such a generic function, it will forget the old method
associated to this generic function. Using the MOP (Meta Object Protocol) is
quite easy, as we will see below |#

;;; First define a new class <unique-generic>
(define-class <unique-generic> (<generic>)
  ())

;;;; The MOP says that add-method will be called when a new method is
;;;; defined. Parameters are the generic function and the new method.
;;;; Define a new method for <unique-generic>
;;;;
(define-method add-method! ((gf <unique-generic>) (m <method>))
  ;; Do the normal stuff (add the method to the class involved in the
  ;; specializers of the method + other method and classes bookkeeping.
  (next-method)
  ;; Set the list of methods of this generic to a list containing only m
  (slot-set! gf 'methods (list m)))


;;;; OPTIONAL: This is needed to have better messages on errors
;;;;
;;;; Note that when the function is called with bad parameters
;;;; we will have the message
;;;;      no applicable method for #[<generic> log (1)]
;;;;      in call (log bad usage)
;;;; We can redefine this message to hide a bit the implementation
;;;; details with a new method for no-applicable-method:
(define-method no-applicable-method ((gf <unique-generic>) args)
  (error 'not-applicable
         "invalid call ~S\nType of accepted parameters: ~S"
         (cons (generic-function-name gf) args)
         (map* class-name
               (method-specializers (car (generic-function-methods gf))))))

;;;;
;;;; Everything is ready. Here we go
;;;;

;;;; define log as a generic of class <unique-generic>
(define-generic log <unique-generic>)

(define-method log ([msg <string>] value)
  (eprintf "*** ~A: ~W~%" msg value))

;;;; ----------------------------------------------------------------------
(define (main args)
  ;; This call will succeed
  (printf "A successful call\n")
  (log "A test" 100)
  ;; This one will not
  (with-handler (lambda (c)
                  (printf "A bad call. Message was:\n~A\n"
                          (condition-message c)))
                (log 'bad 'usage)))
